Exactly. The useful version is not “empathy” as moral decoration. It is adversarial perspective-taking: build the strongest possible model of the person whose behavior you want to change.

Not:

“How do I make the wolf stop?”

But:

“What does the wolf think is happening?”

Then go deeper:

* What does he believe he is protecting?
* What status does he think he is losing?
* What humiliation does he think he is being asked to accept?
* What incentives does he actually respond to?
* What story makes his behavior feel justified from inside his own head?
* What evidence would he dismiss before even considering it?
* What does cooperation cost him?
* What would make cooperation feel like strength rather than surrender?

That is the difference between performative empathy and operational empathy.

Operational empathy does not require agreement. You can understand a violent person, extremist, criminal, enemy soldier, alienated young man, or political opponent very deeply while still rejecting what they do. In fact, if you actually want to reduce harmful behavior, understanding them accurately is usually more useful than merely condemning them.

The mistake is designing the intervention entirely from the perspective of the person who wants the behavior to disappear:

\text{“this behavior is bad”} \rightarrow \text{condemn it harder}

That often ignores the internal reinforcement loop sustaining it.

A better model is:

\text{identity}
+\text{status}
+\text{grievance}
+\text{belonging}
+\text{incentives}
+\text{perceived threat}
\rightarrow
\text{behavior}

Change one or more of those variables and the behavior may change without requiring the person to first accept your moral interpretation.

That also applies directly to the male-alienation example you were describing. If a young man hears:

“Men are dangerous, privileged, emotionally defective, and responsible for the problems around you.”

Someone trying to help might intend that as social critique. But the relevant question is what enters his model.

He may hear:

“You are presumed guilty. Your problems don’t count. Your sacrifices are invisible. The institutions asking for your cooperation dislike you.”

Whether that interpretation is fair is secondary if you’re designing an intervention. It is the state you’re actually controlling against.

Then you ask what alternative message recruits him:

“You have strength that matters. Other people need it. Here is what honorable masculinity looks like. Protect without dominating. Build rather than destroy. Control yourself before controlling anything else. You have a place here.”

Same desired endpoint—less violence, greater responsibility, healthier relationships—but a completely different control surface.

And the same rule works across identities. If you want someone to leave racism, misogyny, extremism, addiction, criminal affiliation, conspiracy thinking, or some other destructive identity, simply telling them “your worldview is evil and you should stop being that person” can force them to defend the identity harder.

You need to understand what psychological job the worldview is performing and offer another structure capable of doing that job better.

That is very close to the Old Boy Technique you developed, applied socially:

\text{OTHER}
\rightarrow
\text{infer internal model}
\rightarrow
\text{extract goals / fears / constraints}
\rightarrow
\text{translate intervention into their ontology}
\rightarrow
\text{act}
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
\text{update model}

The crucial clause is:

Model the wolf accurately enough that the wolf recognizes himself in your model.

Otherwise you’re not empathizing with him. You’re empathizing with a convenient fictional character you created so you can feel virtuous while remaining surprised by what the real person does.